  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 513 AM MDT Sun Oct 18 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nIn the wake of Saturdays high wind event, a much calmer regime takes \nhold as upper-level ridging builds overhead. Generally calmer, light \nwinds will take hold as near normal temperatures settle in across \nthe area Sunday through Tuesday.\n\nBy midweek, split flow establishes over the western CONUS, leaving a \ncutoff low centered off the coast of California and allowing \nstronger ridging to expand westward across the northern Rockies and \nnorthern Plains. This pattern induces southwesterly flow aloft over \nMontana, driving a warming trend Wednesday through the end of the \nworkweek. The southwesterly flow will also channel increased \nmoisture into the area, resulting in the return of daily chances of \nafternoon showers largely focused across Southwest Montana Tuesday \nthrough Thursday. \n\nHeading into next weekend, the cutoff low off the California coast \nbegins to re-merge with the main jet stream thanks to a digging \ntrough that originates from Alaska dips southward over the \nPacific Northwest. Model consensus favors this feature \ntransitioning into an open shortwave as it pushes east across the \nregion. This progression will bring increased chances for more \nwidespread precipitation alongside a drop in high temperature back\ndown to near or slightly below seasonal averages. Winds through \nthe entire forecast period are expected to remain generally light,\nwith the exception of Wednesday which will see slightly breezier \nconditions as we advect warmer air in from the south/southeast.\n\n-JR\n\n-Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nConfidence is high for light winds and near-normal temperatures \nthrough Tuesday under weak ridging, followed by above-normal \ntemperatures Wednesday through Friday as the ridge axis expands \nfurther west. Confidence is also moderate to high for scattered, \ndiurnally driven afternoon showers remaining largely confined to \nSouthwest Montana Tuesday through Thursday. \n\nConfidence is low regarding next weekends system due to divergence \namong both deterministic and ensemble guidance on how the cutoff \nlow interacts with the broader jet:             \n\n    -Scenario 1 (Open Progressive Shortwave - Favored): The \n     cutoff low phases smoothly into the northern branch as an \n     open wave, bringing a fairly quick round of more widespread \n     precipitation and cooling temperatures back near or slightly \n     below average. \n\n    -Scenario 2 (Slower/Deeper phasing): If the cutoff low lingers\n     longer off the coast, or phases more aggressively with\n     incoming norther-stream energy, precipitation timing could\n     slow down, potentially prolonging moisture streaming from the\n     southwest and bringing a sharper drop in temperatures once\n     the eventual troughing pushes through. \n\n-JR",
